README.OSK -- cpucache, "On-chip caches on Motorola processors"Carsten Emde's cache-control program from OS-9 International issue 3/94,page 23, with the article itself as DOC/cpucache/cache.txt.  cpucache showsthe 68020/68030/68040 cache control register and switches the data andinstruction caches on and off (F$CCtl); -t also shows the 68040'stransparent-translation registers.  cctrap is the system-state trap handlerit reads CACR and the TT registers through.The code disk's package had four modules.  Two are here:  cpucache   the program (CMDS/OBJS/cpucache on the code disk)  cctrap     its trap handlerAnd two are not:  sccctl     an SCF driver whose source says it is "the proprietary             confidential property of Carsten Emde ... Reproduction,             publication, or distribution in any form ... is prohibited"             (DRVR/sccctl.a, lines 4-10) -- the same words Microware used.  cctl       its descriptor, built from Microware's scfdesc.a (DESC/),             which is theirs and says so.DEFS/oskdefs.d (Microware's, lightly edited) and the built libraries areleft out too.WITHOUT /cctl, cpucache's report stops at "can't open cctl device"; whatworks without it is `cpucache -q on|off', which switches the caches andprints nothing.  CMDS/cpucache on the code disk was all four modulesmerged into one file; it is not used here.Changes: none to any file shipped.Not tested: on this emulator, with cctrap loaded, cpucache stops with aprivilege violation -- reading CACR is a supervisor instruction and needs areal 68020 or later.